from ../plugins import Plugins
from ../plugins/Reach import PluginReach
from ./TestCommand import TestCommand

export anchor Testing:
    description:
        A test is a statement about the project, written in plain English,
        that an agent judges true or false. "The Save button saves the
        document" is a test. It isn't run as code: `piton test` hands the
        statement to an agentic tool, which reads the specification, the code,
        or both, and says whether the statement holds.

        Tests check what the specification and the implementation say, which
        a compiler can't. Compiling proves guidance is well-formed; a test asks
        whether it, or the code built from it, means what it should.

    plugin:
        Testing is a plugin, described in @{Plugins}, bundled with the
        compiler: the test keyword is its Piton files, @piton/test, and
        `piton test` is its command, written in TypeScript. Its source is in
        the compiler's repository at plugins/test, its Piton files in pi, its
        declaration in plugin.pi, and its code in src. It is the reference for
        writing a plugin, and nothing in it is special to being bundled. It
        requires Belay, whose exports give it the agentic tools and the
        fluency prompt each is launched with.

    keyword: @{TestKeyword}
    configuration: @{TestConfiguration}
    command: @{TestCommand}

export anchor TestKeyword:
    description:
        The test keyword comes from @piton/test, the test plugin's Piton files,
        which every project has, like @piton/config and @piton/belay. A file
        uses it with `use @piton/test`.

        ```piton
        \\\
        use @piton/test

        export test SaveButtonSaves:
            expects: The Save button saves the document
            against: code
            tags: [editor, save]
        \\\
        ```

    shape:
        ```piton
        \\\
        export abstract anchor Test as test:
            expects:: string
            against:: string:: null: null
            description:: string:: null: null
            tags:: string[]:: null: null
        \\\
        ```

    expects:
        Required. The statement, in plain English. It passes when the agent
        judges it true of what it reads, and fails otherwise. A failure comes
        with a short explanation of why, written in ASD-STE100 Simplified
        Technical English, and a prompt to give an agent to fix it.

    against:
        Optional. What the statement is judged against: spec, code, or both.
        Left out, the project's default is used, and without one, both.
        Against both, the statement has to hold in the specification and in
        the code, so a test passes only when they agree with it.

    descriptionProperty:
        Optional. Context for the agent, such as where to look or what the
        statement means by a word. It never changes what is being asserted.

    tags:
        Optional. A list of words to select tests by, like
        `\ tags: [save, editor] \`. Each is trimmed, and an empty one is
        dropped. They mean nothing to the agent and are not sent to it; they
        are only for choosing which tests a run includes, with an expression
        like `piton test --tags "editor not slow"`.

    discovery:
        Every exported test anchor in the project's source root is a test,
        whether or not anything reaches it from the entry, so tests can live
        beside what they describe without the build writing them out. A test
        that isn't exported is not run, which is how one is kept without
        running it. Tests in installed packages under tethers/
        are not the project's and are not run.

        The test runner reaches every test, though nothing in the specbase
        leads to it. So the test plugin declares reach: true, and its reach
        hook returns each test it discovers, as @{PluginReach} describes. `piton
        reach` and the language server count the tests as roots, so a test is
        never reported as unreachable or unused.

export anchor TestConfiguration:
    description:
        A project sets its defaults for `piton test` with a test-config anchor
        from @piton/test, the test plugin's settings, listed under plugins in
        piton.config.pi.

        ```piton
        \\\
        use @piton/config
        use @piton/test

        export piton-config Config:
            root: ./spec
            plugins:
                - {Tests}

        test-config Tests:
            against: spec
            agent: codex
            model: gpt-6.1-sol
            jobs: 8
        \\\
        ```

    shape:
        ```piton
        \\\
        export abstract anchor TestConfig as test-config:
            against:: string:: null: null
            agent:: string:: null: null
            model:: string:: null: null
            jobs:: number:: null: null
        \\\
        ```

    against:
        spec, code, or both: what a test that doesn't say is judged against.
        Both when left out.

    agent:
        Which tool judges the tests: claude, codex, opencode, or muse, the
        tools `piton agent` launches. Claude when left out.

    model:
        The model that judges the tests, as the tool names it. The tool's own
        default when left out, and then the run lists the tool's models.

    jobs:
        How many tests run at once, a whole number of at least 1. One runs
        them one after another. Four when left out.

    precedence:
        A flag on the command line wins over the test, and the test wins over
        the configuration, so a single run can be pointed at the code without
        editing anything, and a test that only makes sense against the
        specification can say so.

    errors:
        A value that isn't one of the choices is an error that names the
        choices, whether it is in the settings, in a test, or on the command
        line, and nothing is run. In the settings it is reported where it is
        written, with the source it points at: invalid-test-scope for
        against, unknown-test-agent for agent, invalid-test-model for a model
        that is not a name, and invalid-jobs for jobs.
